WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:07.128
In the summer of 2014,

00:00:07.128 --> 00:00:12.926
I was in Iraqi Kurdistan
with a small team of archaeologists,

00:00:12.926 --> 00:00:18.098
finishing a season of field excavations
near the border town of Halabja.

00:00:18.932 --> 00:00:24.729
Our project was looking into something
which has puzzled and intrigued me

00:00:24.729 --> 00:00:27.774
ever since I began studying archeology.

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:31.403
We're taught to believe
that thousands of years ago,

00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:35.073
when our ancestors
first invented agriculture

00:00:35.073 --> 00:00:36.783
in that part of the world,

00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:40.036
that it set in motion
a chain of consequences

00:00:40.078 --> 00:00:44.499
that would shape our modern world
in a particular direction,

00:00:44.499 --> 00:00:47.002
on a particular course.

00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:49.713
By farming wheat,

00:00:49.754 --> 00:00:55.051
our ancestors supposedly developed
new attachments to the land they lived on.

00:00:55.093 --> 00:00:57.178
Private property was invented.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.931
And with that, the need to defend it.

00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:05.604
Along with new opportunities
for some people to accumulate surpluses,

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:07.814
came new labor demands,

00:01:07.856 --> 00:01:13.737
tying most people to a hard regime
of tending their crops

00:01:13.737 --> 00:01:17.741
while a privileged few received freedom

00:01:17.741 --> 00:01:20.869
and the leisure to do other things.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.913
To think, to experiment,

00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:28.835
to create the foundations
of what we refer to as civilization.

00:01:29.544 --> 00:01:31.796
Now, according to this familiar story,

00:01:31.838 --> 00:01:35.717
what happened next
is that populations boomed,

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:39.429
villages turned into towns,
towns became cities,

00:01:39.471 --> 00:01:41.473
and with the emergence of cities,

00:01:41.514 --> 00:01:46.978
our species was locked on a familiar
trajectory of development

00:01:46.978 --> 00:01:52.067
where spiraling populations
and technological change

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:56.613
were bound up with the kind
of dreadful inequalities

00:01:56.655 --> 00:01:59.282
that we see around us today.

00:02:01.242 --> 00:02:04.162
Except, as anyone can tell you,

00:02:04.204 --> 00:02:07.666
who's looked at the evidence
from the Middle East,

00:02:07.707 --> 00:02:12.921
almost nothing of what I've just
been saying is actually true.

00:02:14.756 --> 00:02:17.801
And the consequences I'm going to suggest

00:02:17.801 --> 00:02:19.344
are quite profound.

00:02:20.428 --> 00:02:23.473
Actually, what happened
after the invention of agriculture

00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:25.934
around 10,000 years ago,

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:29.938
is a long period of around
another 4,000 years

00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:33.483
in which villages
largely remained villages.

00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:39.322
And actually there's very little evidence
for the emergence of rigid social classes,

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:42.033
which is not to say that nothing happened.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.993
Over those 4,000 years,

00:02:44.035 --> 00:02:48.123
technological change
actually proceeded apace.

00:02:48.623 --> 00:02:50.291
Without kings,

00:02:50.291 --> 00:02:53.086
without bureaucracies,
without standing armies,

00:02:53.128 --> 00:02:58.550
these early farming populations fostered
the development of mathematical knowledge,

00:02:58.591 --> 00:03:00.427
advanced metallurgy.

00:03:00.468 --> 00:03:04.723
They learned to cultivate olives,
vines and date palms.

00:03:04.764 --> 00:03:08.476
They invented leavened bread, beer,

00:03:08.476 --> 00:03:10.603
and they developed textile technologies:

00:03:10.603 --> 00:03:12.856
the potter's wheel, the sail.

00:03:13.273 --> 00:03:16.609
And they spread all of these
innovations far and wide,

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:19.362
from the shores
of the eastern Mediterranean,

00:03:19.362 --> 00:03:20.947
up to the Black Sea,

00:03:20.947 --> 00:03:22.991
and from the Persian Gulf,

00:03:22.991 --> 00:03:27.704
all the way over
to the mountains of Kurdistan,

00:03:27.746 --> 00:03:31.332
where our excavations were taking place.

00:03:32.208 --> 00:03:36.838
I've often referred, half jokingly,
to this long period of human history

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.508
as the era of the first global village.

00:03:40.925 --> 00:03:45.096
Because it's not just the technological
innovations that are so remarkable,

00:03:45.138 --> 00:03:46.973
but also the social innovations

00:03:47.015 --> 00:03:50.018
which enabled people
to do all these things

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:52.145
without forming centers

00:03:52.145 --> 00:03:57.942
and without raising up a class
of permanent leaders over everybody else.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.487
Now, oddly enough,

00:04:00.528 --> 00:04:06.242
this efflorescence of culture
is not what we usually refer to

00:04:06.284 --> 00:04:08.036
as civilization.

00:04:08.995 --> 00:04:15.668
Instead, that term is usually reserved
for harshly unequal societies,

00:04:15.668 --> 00:04:18.004
which came thousands of years later.

00:04:18.046 --> 00:04:21.466
Dynastic Mesopotamia. Pharaonic Egypt.

00:04:21.508 --> 00:04:22.842
Imperial Rome.

00:04:22.884 --> 00:04:26.513
Societies that were deeply stratified.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.140
So in short,

00:04:29.182 --> 00:04:32.852
I've always felt that there was
basically something very weird

00:04:32.852 --> 00:04:36.022
about our concept of civilization,

00:04:36.064 --> 00:04:39.776
something that leaves us
lost for words, tongue tied.

00:04:39.818 --> 00:04:43.238
When we're confronted with thousands
of years of human beings,

00:04:43.238 --> 00:04:47.826
say, practicing agriculture,
creating new technologies,

00:04:47.826 --> 00:04:50.370
but not lording it over each other

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.414
or exploiting each other to the maximum.

00:04:54.207 --> 00:04:55.708
Why don't we have better words?

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:00.421
Where is our lexicon for those long
expanses of human history

00:05:00.421 --> 00:05:02.841
in which we weren't behaving that way?

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:06.594
Over the past ten years or more,

00:05:06.594 --> 00:05:10.098
I worked closely together with the late,

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:14.185
great anthropologist David Graeber

00:05:14.185 --> 00:05:16.980
to address some of these questions.

00:05:17.021 --> 00:05:18.857
But we did it on a much larger scale

00:05:18.857 --> 00:05:24.028
because from our perspective
as an archaeologist and an anthropologist,

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:27.740
this clash between theory and data,

00:05:27.782 --> 00:05:30.368
between the standard narrative
of human history

00:05:30.368 --> 00:05:33.413
and the evidence
that we have before us today

00:05:33.413 --> 00:05:36.624
is not just confined
to the early Middle East.

00:05:37.458 --> 00:05:38.793
It’s everything:

00:05:38.835 --> 00:05:42.922
out whole picture of human history
that we’ve been telling for centuries,

00:05:43.381 --> 00:05:45.216
it’s basically wrong.

00:05:45.216 --> 00:05:49.262
I'm going to try and explain
a few more of the reasons why.

00:05:49.637 --> 00:05:52.473
Let's go back to some
of those core concepts,

00:05:52.515 --> 00:05:57.145
the stable reference points
around which we've been organizing

00:05:57.186 --> 00:06:02.191
and orchestrating our understanding
of world history for hundreds of years.

00:06:03.026 --> 00:06:06.905
Take, for instance, that notion
that for most of its history,

00:06:06.905 --> 00:06:12.243
the human species lived in tiny
egalitarian bands of hunter gatherers,

00:06:12.285 --> 00:06:17.832
until the advent of agriculture
ushered in a new age of inequality.

00:06:18.875 --> 00:06:24.839
Or the notion that with the arrival
of cities came social classes,

00:06:24.881 --> 00:06:31.596
sacred kings and rapacious oligarchs
trampling everyone else underfoot.

00:06:32.055 --> 00:06:34.599
From our very first history lessons,

00:06:34.641 --> 00:06:38.019
we're taught to believe
that our modern world,

00:06:38.061 --> 00:06:41.356
with all of its advantages and amenities,

00:06:41.397 --> 00:06:43.608
modern health care, space travel,

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.444
all the things that are good and exciting,

00:06:46.486 --> 00:06:48.655
couldn't possibly exist

00:06:48.655 --> 00:06:52.033
without that original
concentration of humanity

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:54.786
into larger and larger units

00:06:54.786 --> 00:07:00.333
and the relentless buildup
of inequalities that came with it.

00:07:00.333 --> 00:07:04.253
Inequality, we're taught to believe,

00:07:04.253 --> 00:07:08.091
was the necessary price of civilization.

00:07:09.217 --> 00:07:13.179
Well, if so, then what are we to make
of the early Middle East?

00:07:13.221 --> 00:07:16.975
Perhaps one might say there was just
a very, very, very long lag time,

00:07:17.016 --> 00:07:18.810
4,000 years,

00:07:18.851 --> 00:07:20.937
before all these developments took place.

00:07:20.937 --> 00:07:23.940
Inequality was bound to happen,
it was bound to set in.

00:07:24.273 --> 00:07:25.942
It was just a matter of time.

00:07:26.693 --> 00:07:29.570
And perhaps the rest
of the story still works

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.364
for other parts of the world.

00:07:32.532 --> 00:07:37.120
Well, let's think a bit about what
we can actually say today

00:07:37.161 --> 00:07:39.288
about the origin of cities.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:43.668
Surely, you might think,
with the appearance of cities

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.671
came the appearance of social classes.

00:07:46.671 --> 00:07:51.384
Think about ancient Egypt
with its pyramid temples.

00:07:51.426 --> 00:07:55.179
Or Shang China with its lavish tombs.

00:07:55.179 --> 00:07:58.391
The classic Maya
with their warlike rulers.

00:07:59.142 --> 00:08:03.187
Or the Inca empire with
its mummified kings and queens.

00:08:03.938 --> 00:08:06.941
But actually, the picture
these days is not so clear.

00:08:06.983 --> 00:08:10.361
What modern archeology
tells us, for example,

00:08:10.403 --> 00:08:12.822
is that there were already cities

00:08:12.822 --> 00:08:15.408
on the lower reaches of the Yellow River

00:08:15.408 --> 00:08:19.078
over 1,000 years
before the rise of the Shang.

00:08:19.495 --> 00:08:21.456
And on the other side of the Pacific,

00:08:21.497 --> 00:08:24.042
in Peru’s Rio Supe,

00:08:24.083 --> 00:08:27.211
we already see huge
agglomerations of people

00:08:27.253 --> 00:08:32.633
with monumental architecture
4,000 years before the Inca.

00:08:33.926 --> 00:08:36.679
In South Asia, 4,500 years ago,

00:08:36.721 --> 00:08:41.059
the first cities appeared
at places like Mohenjo-daro

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:43.394
and Harappa in the Indus Valley.

00:08:43.895 --> 00:08:50.401
But these huge settlements present
no evidence of kings or queens.

00:08:52.487 --> 00:08:56.157
No royal monuments, no aggrandizing art.

00:08:56.199 --> 00:08:59.869
And what's more, we know
that much of the population

00:08:59.911 --> 00:09:03.998
lived in high-quality housing
with excellent sanitation.

00:09:04.415 --> 00:09:06.459
North of the Black Sea,

00:09:06.459 --> 00:09:08.920
in the modern country of Ukraine,

00:09:08.961 --> 00:09:12.799
archaeologists have found evidence
of even more ancient cities

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.134
going back 6,000 years.

00:09:15.635 --> 00:09:16.886
And again,

00:09:16.928 --> 00:09:20.932
these huge settlements present no evidence

00:09:20.973 --> 00:09:24.060
of authoritarian rule.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.188
No temples, no palaces,

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:29.857
not even any evidence
of central storage facilities

00:09:29.899 --> 00:09:32.026
or top-down bureaucracy.

00:09:32.944 --> 00:09:37.907
Actually what we see in those cases
are these great concentric rings of houses

00:09:37.949 --> 00:09:41.077
arranged rather like
the inside of a tree trunk

00:09:41.119 --> 00:09:43.621
around neighborhood assembly halls.

00:09:44.038 --> 00:09:49.335
And it stayed that way
for about 800 years.

00:09:51.838 --> 00:09:58.052
So what this means is that long before
the birth of democracy in ancient Greece,

00:09:58.094 --> 00:10:01.139
there were already well-organized cities

00:10:01.139 --> 00:10:03.474
on several of the world's continents

00:10:03.474 --> 00:10:07.687
which present no evidence
for ruling dynasties.

00:10:08.146 --> 00:10:13.109
And some of them also seem to have managed
perfectly well without priests,

00:10:13.151 --> 00:10:15.820
mandarins and warrior politicians.

00:10:16.362 --> 00:10:19.240
Of course, some early cities did go on

00:10:19.282 --> 00:10:22.660
to become the capitals
of kingdoms and empires.

00:10:23.161 --> 00:10:25.413
But it's important to note
that others went

00:10:25.454 --> 00:10:28.416
in completely the opposite direction.

00:10:29.584 --> 00:10:32.003
To take one well-documented example,

00:10:32.003 --> 00:10:35.548
around the year 250 AD,

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:39.677
the city of Teotihuacan,
in the valley of Mexico,

00:10:39.719 --> 00:10:43.556
with a population
of around 100,000 people,

00:10:43.598 --> 00:10:48.728
turned its back on pyramid temples
and human sacrifices

00:10:48.728 --> 00:10:51.647
and reconstituted itself

00:10:51.689 --> 00:10:58.613
as a vast collection of comfortable villas
housing most of the city's population.

00:10:59.488 --> 00:11:03.451
When archaeologists first
investigated these buildings,

00:11:03.492 --> 00:11:05.328
they assumed they were palaces.

00:11:05.328 --> 00:11:08.581
Then they realized that just
about everyone in the city

00:11:08.623 --> 00:11:11.876
was living in a palace
with spacious patios

00:11:11.876 --> 00:11:13.544
and subfloor drainages,

00:11:13.586 --> 00:11:16.088
gorgeous murals on the walls.

00:11:16.631 --> 00:11:18.591
But we shouldn't get carried away.

00:11:19.383 --> 00:11:22.386
None of the societies
that I've been describing

00:11:22.428 --> 00:11:25.431
was perfectly egalitarian.

00:11:26.641 --> 00:11:30.394
But then we might also remember
that fifth-century Athens,

00:11:30.394 --> 00:11:33.773
which we look to
as the birthplace of democracy,

00:11:33.773 --> 00:11:40.404
was also a militaristic society
founded on chattel slavery,

00:11:40.404 --> 00:11:44.533
where women were completely
excluded from politics.

00:11:45.368 --> 00:11:46.661
So maybe by comparison,

00:11:46.702 --> 00:11:49.872
somewhere like Teotihuacan
was not doing so badly

00:11:49.872 --> 00:11:53.876
at keeping the genie
of inequality in its bottle.

00:11:53.918 --> 00:11:56.879
But maybe we can just forget
about all that, we can look away.

00:11:56.879 --> 00:12:01.634
Perhaps all of these things
I'm talking about are basically outliers.

00:12:03.052 --> 00:12:07.515
Maybe we can still keep
our familiar story of civilization intact.

00:12:08.933 --> 00:12:10.142
And after all,

00:12:10.142 --> 00:12:16.190
if cities without rulers were really
such a common thing in human history,

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:21.112
why didn't Cortéz and Pizarro
and all the other conquistadors

00:12:21.153 --> 00:12:24.657
find any when they began
their invasion of the Americas?

00:12:24.699 --> 00:12:29.036
Why did they find only
Moctezuma and Atahualpa

00:12:29.078 --> 00:12:31.163
lording it over their empires?

00:12:32.498 --> 00:12:34.583
Except that's not true either.

00:12:35.042 --> 00:12:39.588
Actually, the city where Hernan Cortéz
found his military allies,

00:12:39.588 --> 00:12:42.425
the ones who enabled
his successful assault

00:12:42.466 --> 00:12:45.970
on the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlán,

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:50.391
was exactly one such city without rulers:

00:12:50.975 --> 00:12:55.313
an indigenous republic
by the name of Tlaxcala,

00:12:55.354 --> 00:12:57.523
governed by an urban parliament,

00:12:57.565 --> 00:13:00.651
which had some pretty interesting
initiation rituals

00:13:00.693 --> 00:13:02.278
for would-be politicians.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.280
They'd be periodically whipped

00:13:04.322 --> 00:13:07.742
and subject to public abuse
by their constituents

00:13:07.742 --> 00:13:14.582
to sort of break down their egos
and remind them who's really in charge.

00:13:14.999 --> 00:13:19.337
It's a little bit different from what
we expect of our politicians today.

00:13:19.337 --> 00:13:23.883
And archaeologists, by the way,
have also worked at this place Tlaxcala,

00:13:23.924 --> 00:13:26.969
excavating the remains
of the pre-conquest city,

00:13:27.011 --> 00:13:29.221
and what they found there
is really remarkable.

00:13:29.263 --> 00:13:33.809
Again, the most impressive architecture
is not temples and palaces.

00:13:33.809 --> 00:13:37.855
It's just the well-appointed residences
of ordinary citizens

00:13:37.855 --> 00:13:44.153
arrayed along these grand terraces
overlooking district plazas.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.323
And it's not just the history of cities

00:13:47.365 --> 00:13:52.036
that modern archaeological science
is turning on its head.

00:13:52.995 --> 00:13:55.790
We also know now that the history
of human societies

00:13:55.831 --> 00:13:57.625
before the coming of agriculture

00:13:57.666 --> 00:14:00.419
is just nothing
like what we once imagined.

00:14:01.337 --> 00:14:03.923
Far from this idea of people
living all the time

00:14:03.964 --> 00:14:06.008
in tiny bands of hunter-gatherers,

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:07.885
actually, what we see these days

00:14:07.885 --> 00:14:13.432
is evidence for a really wild variety
of social experimentation

00:14:13.474 --> 00:14:15.393
before the coming of farming.

00:14:15.393 --> 00:14:16.644
In Africa,

00:14:16.644 --> 00:14:18.854
50,000 years ago,

00:14:18.854 --> 00:14:23.275
hunter-gatherers were already
creating huge networks,

00:14:23.317 --> 00:14:26.529
social networks, covering
large parts of the continent.

00:14:26.946 --> 00:14:30.157
In Ice Age Europe, 25,000 years ago,

00:14:30.199 --> 00:14:36.247
we see evidence of individuals
singled out for special grand burials,

00:14:36.247 --> 00:14:39.542
their bodies suffused with ornamentation,

00:14:39.542 --> 00:14:42.503
weapons and even
what looked like regalia.

00:14:42.503 --> 00:14:48.217
We see public buildings constructed
on the bones and tusks of woolly mammoth.

00:14:49.051 --> 00:14:51.929
And around 11,000 years ago,

00:14:51.929 --> 00:14:54.765
back in the Middle East, where I started,

00:14:54.807 --> 00:14:59.854
hunter-gatherers constructed
enormous stone temples

00:14:59.854 --> 00:15:04.191
at a place called Göbekli Tepe
in eastern Turkey.

00:15:05.234 --> 00:15:06.986
In North America,

00:15:07.027 --> 00:15:09.321
long before the coming of maize farming,

00:15:09.363 --> 00:15:13.993
indigenous populations created
the massive earthworks of poverty point

00:15:14.034 --> 00:15:15.453
in Louisiana,

00:15:15.494 --> 00:15:19.415
capable of hosting hunter gatherer
publics in their thousands.

00:15:19.457 --> 00:15:23.752
And then Japan, again,
long before the arrival of rice farming,

00:15:23.794 --> 00:15:29.091
the storehouses of Sannai Maruyama
could already hold great surpluses

00:15:29.133 --> 00:15:30.968
of wild plant foods.

00:15:30.968 --> 00:15:33.721
Now what do all these details amount to?

00:15:33.762 --> 00:15:35.598
What does it all mean?

00:15:35.639 --> 00:15:37.975
Well, at the very least, I'd suggest

00:15:38.017 --> 00:15:43.314
it's really a bit far-fetched these days
to cling to this notion

00:15:43.355 --> 00:15:50.237
that the invention of agriculture meant
a departure from some egalitarian Eden.

00:15:50.863 --> 00:15:54.575
Or to cling to the idea
that small-scale societies

00:15:54.617 --> 00:15:57.286
are especially likely to be egalitarian,

00:15:57.328 --> 00:16:01.499
while large-scale ones
must necessarily have kings,

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.001
presidents and top-down
structures of management.

00:16:05.127 --> 00:16:07.922
And there are also some
contemporary implications.

00:16:07.963 --> 00:16:10.799
Take, for example, the commonplace notion

00:16:10.799 --> 00:16:16.263
that participatory democracy is somehow
natural in a small community.

00:16:16.597 --> 00:16:18.682
Or perhaps an activist group,

00:16:18.682 --> 00:16:23.771
but couldn't possibly have a scale up
for anything like a city,

00:16:23.812 --> 00:16:25.940
a nation or even a region.

00:16:26.732 --> 00:16:29.193
Well, actually, the evidence
of human history,

00:16:29.193 --> 00:16:31.362
if we're prepared to look at it,

00:16:31.362 --> 00:16:33.030
suggests the opposite.

00:16:33.948 --> 00:16:36.909
If cities and regional confederacies,

00:16:36.951 --> 00:16:41.497
held together mostly
by consensus and cooperation

00:16:41.539 --> 00:16:44.875
existed thousands of years ago,

00:16:44.875 --> 00:16:48.921
who's to stop us creating them again today

00:16:48.921 --> 00:16:52.967
with technologies that allow us
to overcome the friction

00:16:52.967 --> 00:16:55.427
of distance and numbers?

00:16:56.053 --> 00:17:02.142
Perhaps it's not too late to begin
learning from all this new evidence

00:17:02.142 --> 00:17:03.769
of the human past,

00:17:04.144 --> 00:17:06.146
even to begin imagining

00:17:06.188 --> 00:17:10.442
what other kinds of civilization
we might create

00:17:10.442 --> 00:17:14.905
if we can just stop telling ourselves

00:17:14.947 --> 00:17:20.035
that this particular world
is the only one possible.

00:17:21.328 --> 00:17:22.496
Thank you very much.

00:17:22.538 --> 00:17:27.668
(Applause)

